<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hesbaye</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Haspengouw</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hesbaye (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Haspengouw) is a fertile loess plateau in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dyle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the Demer, spreading across the provinces of Liège, Limburg, Namur and Brabant. A Carolingian county in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it later lay largely within the prince-bishopric of Liège and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>. It is known for cereals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet and fruit orchards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hesbaye.jpg" alt="Hesbaye" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerniel_in_Haspengouw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
